


Running `file` shows that `nasm_crack` is a statically linked, non-stripped 64-bit ELF executable written in NASM assembly, meaning all symbol names remain intact and Linux system calls are invoked directly without `libc`[cite: 1].

---

![Strings Output](./images/2-strings.png)

The `strings` output exposes embedded `.data` strings and symbol labels (`_start`, `correct_func`, `passwd`, `input`), immediately highlighting `"supersecret"` as the primary password candidate[cite: 2].

---

![Disas correct_func](./images/3-disas-correct.png)

Disassembling `correct_func` (`0x401000`) confirms that it simply executes a `sys_write` (`eax = 1`) syscall to print `"Correct!"` (`0x402016`) and terminates the process cleanly via `sys_exit` (`eax = 0x3c`)[cite: 3].

---

![Disas _start](./images/4-disas-start.png)

Inside `_start`, the binary prompts the user via `sys_write`, reads up to 16 bytes into `0x402031` (`input`) via `sys_read`, and compares `0xb` (11) bytes between `RDI` (`0x402026` / `passwd`) and `RSI` (`0x402031` / `input`) using `repz cmps` at `0x401077` before jumping to `correct_func`[cite: 4].

---

![Breakpoint](./images/5-breakpoint.png)
![Registers & Memory](./images/6-pwndbg-regs.png)
![RCX Value](./images/7-rcx.png)

Setting a breakpoint at `0x401077` and running with a test input confirms that `RDI` points to `"supersecret"`, `RSI` points to user input, and `RCX` holds `0xb` (11)—which matches the exact 11-byte length of `"supersecret"` while ignoring the trailing `\n` from `sys_read`[cite: 5, 6, 7].

